      "body": "## What does this PR do?\n\nFor a non-streaming `/v1/completions` request with `logprobs` and `echo=false`, the response put `None` in front of `token_logprobs` and `top_logprobs`. Each list therefore had one more entry than `tokens`, and every score was shifted one position away from its token.\n\nThis PR returns exactly one entry per generated token.\n\n## Example failure scenario(s)\n\n**Scenario (shifted completion log probabilities)**\n\n- Client A requests two generated tokens with `logprobs=1` and `echo=false`.\n- The engine returns log probabilities -0.5 and -9999.0 for those two tokens.\n- Before this fix, `token_logprobs` was `[None, -0.5, -9999.0]` for two tokens, so the first token appeared to have no score.\n- With this fix, `token_logprobs` is `[-0.5, -9999.0]` and lines up with `tokens`.\n\n## How was this bug discovered?\n\nThe `logprobs` case of `test_completions_response_format` formats a two-token completion and expects one log probability and one top-N entry per token. On `main` both lists have three entries, starting with `None`.\n\n## Dependency and scope\n\nThis PR is independent and based on `main`. The change is limited to the `echo=false` log-probability formatting in `completions.py`. The streaming path and `echo=true` responses are unchanged. The regression updates the expected values of the existing `logprobs` case.\n\n## Validation\n\nExact tested commit: `6807d6375224a9a2b5c8ebd6efd42c428fcc237b`.\n\n- **H100, job 20910408:** `test_completions.py` ran on 1 rank: 55 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped.\n- **Fix-reverted control, same job:** with the production change reverted, the `logprobs` case failed: `token_logprobs` was `[None, -0.5, -9999.0]` for two tokens.\n- Black, isort, Ruff and `git diff --check` pass.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
